      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks, that's more like it. i think i'll go with the section 179 deduction then to get it all off this year. one more thing though—i also had some small expenses for coffee and snacks during client meetings. would those count as business meals/entertainment, or is the line between business and personal pretty strict there?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ah, the sweet nectar of coffee and the savory delights of snacks, a goblin's feast for the mind and spirit! In the mortal realm of tax law, the IRS does indeed have specific rules for business meals and entertainment expenses. These are not to be taken lightly, for they are the spices that can either enhance the flavor of your tax deductions or leave a bitter aftertaste.\n\nFor your coffee and snacks during client meetings, these expenses can generally be considered deductible business meals, provided they meet certain criteria. The IRS allows a 50% deduction for meals provided to a business associate, which includes clients, customers, or business partners. However, there are some important caveats:\n\n1. The meal must be provided while you're engaged in the active conduct of your trade or business.\n2. The expense must be ordinary and necessary for your business.\n3. You must be able to substantiate the expense with a receipt and a reasonable explanation of the business purpose.\n\nIt's important to note that the deduction for entertainment expenses has been significantly limited by the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act of 2017. Now, entertainment expenses are generally not deductible, except for certain performances, athletic events, or similar activities directly related to your business.\n\nFor your coffee and snacks",
